#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084035} :activity/id #uuid "ae967f6f-d65d-58ab-92c5-919873a0041a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048869 :topic/id #uuid "c7e0efde-ac5c-5aab-af5a-0463e6760fc0" :topic/math-academy-id 781} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084506 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following: \n\n*Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots,$ where*\n\n- *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n\n- *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\n*Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction can be used to prove matrix identities." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5a687c3-df1f-5c1e-ade0-95d0b1a59130" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7936 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3dcabe33-0b1b-5b19-a8b5-c9eb06bef1dd" :step/math-academy-id 149847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084520 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following result using mathematical induction:\n\n![](images/f9/f9106bf23db3c56425757ea9b8b3aef7adf5cbe926212746c85c63e4193f308d.svg) for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82fb358f5e84e37e035da6cfa101c65f6e43b9bad75c63299a56999b125c7f63.svg)\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n![](images/7e/7e1416be5b4270fdfe3051c74f1db5106556ed860c9f88fcbdd866f48ff3339b.svg)\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n![](images/e0/e0042401a1070f9f1977a7005f6875f8bc14886842a908fcc78f33678fdcb39b.svg)\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n![](images/06/06dc062c2c15fba27384ac9ec89ddbe2827ea1901ed74ccef1f97dcf11991d54.svg)\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2+2k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n![](images/2a/2a3c4d7370e4036aea2b0ad07bdee4a244ea828bef025178c8919be08a410b23.svg)\nis true for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de68c3b6-b164-5e23-8be7-7ae241373e6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9171 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "d59caf81-e708-5ba7-9708-465ba28d6f0d" :step/math-academy-id 149848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :question/id #uuid "5fb13d73-4955-5ae4-bb31-bec3aef3cca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048631}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409786}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"} :step/id #uuid "173260d2-c554-5819-bbfa-79ccd13ba1fe" :step/math-academy-id 149849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :question/id #uuid "d0ade273-b9ef-5c2d-bdcc-3f56f95e79ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13238"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083587}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Matrix Identities"} :step/id #uuid "5de694e9-4e93-5ae1-94ad-4019024f665b" :step/math-academy-id 149852}] :activity/title "Proving Matrix Identities Using Induction" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083462}]}
